<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFrameworks>net10.0;net11.0</TargetFrameworks>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <PackageId>CliInvoke.Core</PackageId>
        <Title>CliInvoke.Core</Title>
        <Authors>Alastair Lundy</Authors>
        <Copyright>Copyright (c) Alastair Lundy 2024-2026</Copyright>
        <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/alastairlundy/CliInvoke</RepositoryUrl>
        <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
        <PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>true</PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>
        <PackageTags>shell, pipe, command-line, executable, processconfiguration, wrapper, standard-input, standard-output, standard-error, input, output, error, arguments, invoke, process, invocation, execution, abstractions, testing, mock, dependency-injection</PackageTags>
        <Description>Abstractions and models for running external processes from .NET. Reference CliInvoke.Core in library projects and mock IProcessInvoker in unit tests; install the CliInvoke package for the implementation.</Description>
        <PackageLicenseExpression>MPL-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
        <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile> 
        <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
        <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
        <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
        <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
        <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
        <Version>3.1.0</Version>
        <PackageVersion>3.2.0</PackageVersion>
        <PackageReleaseNotes>
        - All three packages, `CliInvoke.Core`, `CliInvoke`, and `CliInvoke.Specializations`, now ship assets for .NET 10 and .NET 11. Source builds require the .NET 10 and .NET 11 SDKs, and `global.json` pins version 11.0.100.
        - Validation rules and environment-variable comparisons also use less memory. `RequiresExitCodeZero` reuses the same function instead of creating a new one each time. `ProcessConfiguration.Equals` compares environment variables by key instead of comparing sorted lists.
- `AllowExceptionsIfUnexpected` no longer swallows non-cancellation failures that happen near a timeout.
        </PackageReleaseNotes>
        <Product>CliInvoke.Core</Product>
        <AssemblyVersion>3.2.0.0</AssemblyVersion>
        <FileVersion>3.2.0.0</FileVersion>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <PropertyGroup>
        <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="CliInvoke"/>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="CliInvoke.Tests"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Analyzer" PrivateAssets="all" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <Content Include="README.md">
            <Link>README.md</Link>
            <PackagePath>README.md</PackagePath>
            <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
        </Content>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="..\..\.assets\icon.png">
            <Pack>True</Pack>
            <PackagePath>icon.png</PackagePath>
            <Link>icon.png</Link>
        </None>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <EmbeddedResource Update="Internal\Localizations\Resources.resx">
            <Generator>ResXFileCodeGenerator</Generator>
            <LastGenOutput>Resources.Designer.cs</LastGenOutput>
        </EmbeddedResource>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <Compile Update="Internal\Localizations\Resources.Designer.cs">
            <DesignTime>True</DesignTime>
            <AutoGen>True</AutoGen>
            <DependentUpon>Resources.resx</DependentUpon>
        </Compile>
    </ItemGroup>
</Project>
